<node id="Single Quote">
  <data key="d0">Single Quote</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A single quote or double quote character used to break out of double-quoted attribute values in injection attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;A literal character `'` that attempts to terminate attribute values in XML input.&lt;SEP&gt;A common special character used in command injection payloads to enclose commands or interfere with shell interpretation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5bb2d236b1e2b43118e82283180ff198-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eff998e21395157f6ed4ce495ef006aa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-07-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-12-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Application Firewall">
  <data key="d0">Web Application Firewall</data>
  <data key="d1">DefensiveControl</data>
  <data key="d2">A network-based control that examines incoming traffic, including user input, to block malicious payloads before reaching the web application.&lt;SEP&gt;A network security device or application layer filter that inspects and blocks malicious requests to prevent SQL injection attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;A network security device that filters traffic, often used to block request patterns such as excessive or malicious queries.&lt;SEP&gt;A target control that filters suspicious requests before application logic receives them.&lt;SEP&gt;Target defense mechanism that filters or blocks requests based on attributes including HTTP method.&lt;SEP&gt;Target security control that may filter or obscure duplicated parameter behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5f4e64e8a4e13f14c3ff3f3f15491570-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5f4e64e8a4e13f14c3ff3f3f15491570-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4fc774259228cd414282425d0a281e37-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4fc774259228cd414282425d0a281e37-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3be59af692385f8910776899901b815f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3be59af692385f8910776899901b815f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-apit-99-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-conf-06-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580881</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Validation Control">
  <data key="d0">Input Validation Control</data>
  <data key="d1">DefensiveControl</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanism within or surrounding the web application that checks and sanitizes user input before processing or rendering it.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanism within the web application that checks or filters user input before storing or displaying it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-40abae0a95ecce566511731ae40acbbf-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-02-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580851</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Browser Built-in XSS Protections">
  <data key="d0">Browser Built-in XSS Protections</data>
  <data key="d1">DefensiveControl</data>
  <data key="d2">Browser-level security mechanisms intended to prevent XSS execution even if the web application returns unencoded data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unencoded Special Characters">
  <data key="d0">Unencoded Special Characters</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">Special characters in the HTML response output that have not been properly encoded, filtered, or replaced.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reflected Cross-Site Scripting">
  <data key="d0">Reflected Cross-Site Scripting</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A non-persistent XSS weakness where attacker-supplied code is temporarily included and executed by victim's browser after being reflected from server response.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JavaScript Payload">
  <data key="d0">JavaScript Payload</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A common script-based input structure designed to execute malicious code in the client browser environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Validation Failure">
  <data key="d0">Input Validation Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Flaw in application logic where user-supplied input is not properly sanitized or encoded before being reflected in responses.&lt;SEP&gt;A known vulnerability type where the application fails to verify or sanitize user provided input before using it in queries.&lt;SEP&gt;The failure of an application to check or filter user supplied data before using it in database interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Failure of an application to validate or escape user-supplied inputs before processing them in SQL queries.&lt;SEP&gt;A flaw in the target where user input containing SSI directives is accepted and executed instead of being rejected or sanitized.&lt;SEP&gt;A weakness where input handling lacks proper sanitization, allowing injection attempts to reach mail server protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition where user input is not properly sanitized or validated before being used in object assignment or merge operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e673fd83bcb14789984668dec20fbae1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e673fd83bcb14789984668dec20fbae1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b6a59d6cd5ba7ef78db34ebfda033db4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8572f501a826eda55dfbb3563ca66b07-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-913dbc8a4681245bade3cc137a3a5962-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0afc271ddfef476dba107d5eebef758e-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-01-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-3-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-4-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-08-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-10-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-22-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582917</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attacker Scripting Components">
  <data key="d0">Attacker Scripting Components</data>
  <data key="d1">Artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Malicious code snippets such as steal cookies, modify page elements, or install key loggers commonly embedded within XSS payloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a2a918bb83307307b4812469ae0001c-chunk-000</data>
