<node id="Obtain Account Access">
  <data key="d0">Obtain Account Access</data>
  <data key="d1">attackgoal</data>
  <data key="d2">Obtain Account Access is a strategic goal or attack objective that involves an attacker gaining access to a user account. This objective may be pursued as a strategic result or a deliberate attack goal, with the primary aim being to achieve authenticated access to a user account. The access sought is typically valid and authorized, enabling the attacker to operate under the credentials of a legitimate user. This goal can be framed as either a singular or plural objective, depending on the context, with references to access to a single user account or multiple user accounts. The process may involve exploiting vulnerabilities, credential theft, or other methods to bypass authentication mechanisms. The ultimate purpose is to gain control over an account, which can serve as a foothold for further malicious activities such as data exfiltration, privilege escalation, or lateral movement within a system. The term is often used in cybersecurity contexts to describe a key step in an attack chain, emphasizing the importance of securing user accounts and implementing robust access controls.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67f151b5c1ed66e0d6be294a338e261a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67f151b5c1ed66e0d6be294a338e261a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20d020c04f02649ff62131f95e1cf5af-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20d020c04f02649ff62131f95e1cf5af-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">source-429f1cea7765f3e2.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ea557e757697285d.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-3002b69a47310ca1.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-16c3d9d4e9cc3637.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-0d4e246da395857e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-eae76c910437f664.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1787993216</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Obtain Account Access</data>
</node>
<node id="Escalate Privileges">
  <data key="d0">Escalate Privileges</data>
  <data key="d1">attackgoal</data>
  <data key="d2">Escalate Privileges is a strategic goal or result in cybersecurity where an attacker aims to gain higher access rights, elevated privileges, or increased access levels within a target system or network. This objective typically involves moving beyond the initial level of access granted to the attacker—such as a standard user account—to achieve more powerful permissions, such as administrative or root access. The process often entails exploiting system vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, or weak authentication mechanisms to increase the attacker’s control over the target. Escalation may occur through various techniques, including privilege escalation exploits, credential theft, or leveraging misconfigured services. The ultimate aim is to gain higher-level access rights than initially obtained, enabling the attacker to execute commands, access sensitive data, install malware, or further compromise the system. This goal is a critical phase in many cyberattack methodologies, as it allows the attacker to deepen their foothold and expand their capabilities within the target environment. In some cases, the escalation is described as a strategic result where the attacker successfully achieves elevated privileges, while in others, it is framed as an ongoing objective or goal. Despite variations in phrasing, all descriptions converge on the same core concept: the deliberate pursuit of increased access and control over a system or network.</data>
