<node id="Privilege Escalation Path">
  <data key="d0">Privilege Escalation Path</data>
  <data key="d1">attackercapability</data>
  <data key="d2">Privilege Escalation Path refers to an attacker capability that enables the progression from a lower-privilege state to a higher-privilege state within a target system or environment. This capability involves the existence of a known or exploitable pathway—often a sequence of steps, vulnerabilities, or misconfigurations—that allows an attacker to gain elevated privileges. The path may be established through the exploitation of software flaws, improper access controls, or misconfigured permissions, enabling the attacker to move from a compromised lower-privilege account to one with greater system access. In some cases, this escalation is achieved by following a chain of vulnerabilities or by leveraging a reusable access condition that supports privilege progression. The path may be operational, meaning it is actively available and exploitable, or it may be a theoretical route that an attacker can establish. The ultimate goal is to achieve higher privileges, such as administrative or root access, to perform unauthorized actions within the target environment. This capability is critical in cyberattack scenarios, as it often represents a key step in achieving full system compromise.</data>
