<node id="Arbitrary File Read">
  <data key="d0">Arbitrary File Read</data>
  <data key="d1">attackercapability</data>
  <data key="d2">Arbitrary File Read is an attacker capability that enables the reading of any file on the target system without authorization. This vulnerability allows an attacker to access files that may be restricted or sensitive, including configuration files, logs, user data, and system files, depending on the permissions of the process being exploited. The capability is often achieved through insecure input validation, improper access controls, or misconfigured applications that expose file paths or allow directory traversal. In some cases, the attacker can read files accessible by the target process, which may extend beyond the immediate user context to include system-level resources. This ability is commonly associated with web applications, command-line tools, or services that process user-supplied file paths without proper sanitization. The threat is particularly severe when the compromised process runs with elevated privileges, as it may allow access to critical system files. Arbitrary File Read is a reusable attacker access condition that can be exploited in various attack scenarios, including reconnaissance, privilege escalation, and data exfiltration. It is typically categorized as a security flaw that undermines confidentiality and integrity, and it is often documented in vulnerability databases and security advisories. The impact depends on the specific system configuration and the sensitivity of the files involved.</data>
