<node id="Password Hash">
  <data key="d0">Password Hash</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Password Hash is a concrete object or artifact containing a hashed version of a password, typically obtained from a target system or during an attack. It represents a cryptographic transformation of a user's password, stored in a form that is not directly readable but can be used for authentication or offline cracking. These hashes are often extracted from systems, databases, or authentication mechanisms during security assessments or cyberattacks. The artifact may contain one or more password hashes, and in some cases, it includes hashed credentials or user passwords. Password Hashes are frequently used in offline cracking attempts, where attackers attempt to reverse the hash to recover the original password. They may be obtained through various attack vectors such as privilege escalation, system compromise, or credential dumping. The presence of a Password Hash can indicate credential exposure, especially if the hash is captured from a compromised system. In some contexts, Password Hashes are specifically obtained during an attack step and used for further exploitation, including gaining unauthorized access to systems. These artifacts are often stored in formats like NTLM or SHA-1 hashes and may be processed using tools such as hashcat or John the Ripper. The hash value itself is a fixed-length string derived from the original password using a one-way function, making it computationally infeasible to reverse without brute-force or dictionary attacks. While the hash does not reveal the password directly, it can be exploited if the underlying password is weak or commonly used. The term may also refer to a single hashed password or a collection of multiple hashes, depending on the context. In all cases, Password Hash is a critical element in both cybersecurity defense and offensive operations, serving as both a security mechanism and a potential vulnerability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67f151b5c1ed66e0d6be294a338e261a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67f151b5c1ed66e0d6be294a338e261a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20d020c04f02649ff62131f95e1cf5af-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20d020c04f02649ff62131f95e1cf5af-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4fe7170e2d9c9211e7f5a9c957dd660-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2c01c9361375f50dd8673c9eb15cacd0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2c01c9361375f50dd8673c9eb15cacd0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ffd8f37d7d75ed447c448d9b518f200d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6e8b98451fa828a77a410acce1592cf2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8261eb5533257b5d5534c19022f92a0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92c7622093c392d46fd0e476d89c0395-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92c7622093c392d46fd0e476d89c0395-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ebb35df72c979d0b12d18b62e291d3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-00