<node id="Credential Exposure">
  <data key="d0">Credential Exposure</data>
  <data key="d1">attackgoal</data>
  <data key="d2">Credential Exposure is a weakness class that refers to the unintended or unauthorized access to sensitive credentials within a system or application. This vulnerability arises when credentials are exposed due to insecure storage, transmission, handling, or misconfigurations. Such exposure can occur through various mechanisms, including improper storage practices, insecure logging, flawed logic, or insecure handling during development or deployment. The consequences of Credential Exposure often include unauthorized access to systems or data, enabling attackers to exploit the compromised credentials. This weakness may also result from insecure practices, misconfigurations, or flawed design decisions that make credentials accessible to unauthorized parties. In some contexts, Credential Exposure is considered a strategic goal for attackers seeking to obtain user credentials through exploitation. It is categorized as a technical weakness and a vulnerability class, emphasizing its role in cybersecurity risks. The exposure may occur in the application, system, or target environment, and can lead to credential leakage or access by malicious actors. Overall, Credential Exposure represents a critical security concern that underscores the importance of secure credential management, proper configuration, and robust handling practices.</data>
