<node id="Protocol Allowlist">
  <data key="d0">Protocol Allowlist</data>
  <data key="d1">defensivecontrol</data>
  <data key="d2">Protocol Allowlist is a defensive control mechanism designed to restrict the set of allowed communication protocols within a system, thereby preventing unauthorized, malicious, or abusive network activity. It functions by permitting only a predefined list of specific protocols and blocking all others, ensuring that network traffic adheres strictly to approved standards. This control is commonly applied to both inbound and outbound requests, with particular emphasis on server-side and outbound communications, including URL fetching and HTTP requests. By limiting protocol usage, the Protocol Allowlist mitigates risks such as unauthorized access, malicious behavior, misuse, abuse, and various types of attacks that exploit unsupported or unsecured protocols. It serves as a target control that enforces protocol restrictions to prevent unauthorized traffic, malicious requests, and unauthorized actions, thereby reducing the attack surface and enhancing system security. The control can be implemented at different levels, including network traffic, request handling, and communication endpoints, and is often used in conjunction with other security measures to ensure robust protection against threats.</data>
