<node id="SSRF Localhost Probing">
  <data key="d0">SSRF Localhost Probing</data>
  <data key="d1">attacktechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">SSRF Localhost Probing refers to an action or attack technique that involves using Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) to probe services running on the localhost or internal network of a server. This technique leverages a vulnerable application's server-side URL fetching functionality to send requests to internal endpoints, typically those not accessible from the external network. The primary purpose of SSRF Localhost Probing is to test for internal network exposure, identify reachable services, and discover internal endpoints or systems that may be running on the same host. It is often used in security testing to assess the reachability of localhost services and to detect potential vulnerabilities such as misconfigured firewalls, exposed internal APIs, or services with weak authentication. In some cases, this probing may be conducted to extract sensitive data from internal systems or to evaluate the potential for privilege escalation. The technique is commonly employed by attackers or security researchers to map internal network structures and assess the security posture of a system from within. While it can be used for legitimate penetration testing, it is also a recognized method in cyberattack scenarios where unauthorized access to internal resources is sought. The probing may involve sending crafted requests through SSRF-vulnerable endpoints to determine whether specific services on localhost are accessible, thereby revealing internal infrastructure details. This action highlights the risks associated with improper handling of server-side requests and underscores the importance of input validation and network segmentation in mitigating such threats.</data>
