<node id="Deserialization Gadget Execution">
  <data key="d0">Deserialization Gadget Execution</data>
  <data key="d1">attacktechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Deserialization Gadget Execution is an action that exploits insecure deserialization or deserialization vulnerabilities to execute arbitrary or malicious code. This process typically involves the manipulation of serialized data from untrusted sources, where the deserialization mechanism interprets the data in a way that triggers the execution of unintended code. The attack leverages weaknesses in how applications deserialize objects, particularly when custom or complex object graphs are involved, allowing an attacker to execute code with the privileges of the application. In some contexts, this action is used as a testing technique to identify and validate vulnerabilities in software systems, particularly in environments where deserialization of untrusted data is performed. The execution often occurs through gadget chains—sequences of method calls in libraries that are triggered during deserialization—which enable control over the program’s execution flow. This technique is commonly associated with frameworks and languages that support object serialization, such as Java, .NET, and Python. The primary risk lies in the execution of malicious code, which can lead to unauthorized access, data theft, or system compromise. The action is not limited to a single use case but can be applied both in offensive security testing and real-world exploitation scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2c01c9361375f50dd8673c9eb15cacd0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-02d8de5bc4d7ef5b5730e6de21983a27-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-02d8de5bc4d7ef5b5730e6de21983a27-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">source-4c91a7e4da4eea3a.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-516d729c800024a5.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-4dbc50aa9b3261a0.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-12ff71155a138664.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-0d4e246da395857e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-a9ccf5fa80acec79.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1787995362</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Deserialization Gadget Execution</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Review Access">
  <data key="d0">Source Review Access</data>
  <data key="d1">attackercapability</data>
  <data key="d2">Source Review Access refers to an attacker capability that enables the inspection of source code, typically within the context of a target system or application. This access allows the attacker to review code repositories, version control systems, or exposed endpoints where source code is stored. The ability may be achieved through various means, including unsecured access points, misconfigured systems, or compromised credentials. Once access is obtained, the attacker can examine the code for vulnerabilities, such as insecure coding practices, hardcoded secrets, or logic flaws, which could be exploited for further attacks. This capability is often reusable and operational, meaning it can be leveraged repeatedly across different systems or during extended reconnaissance phases. It is particularly significant in environments where source code is publicly accessible or improperly protected, such as in open repositories or poorly secured development environments. The ability to review source code may also extend to configuration files, further increasing the potential attack surface. Overall, Source Review Access represents a critical threat in cybersecurity, as it provides adversaries with the means to gather intelligence and identify weaknesses before launching targeted exploits.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20d020c04f02649ff62131f95e1cf5af-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2c01c9361375f50dd8673c9eb15cacd0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2c01c9361375f50dd8673c9eb15cacd0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ffd8f37d7d75ed447c448d9b518f200d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92c7622093c392d46fd0e476d89c0395-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92c7622093c392d46fd0e476d89c0395-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1a922685f9944c8d348cfa4732a95f15-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1a922685f9944c8d348cfa4732a95f15-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ca4ebbe397b88a5e1985154047a1fd42-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ca4ebbe397b88a5e1985154047a1fd42-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b7c72285e15bcd2791980b58faff89f6-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f6a368a385f7056f3a4e83a6daacc92e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f6a368a385f7056f3a4e83a6daacc92e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b9fbca654baa1f52872a4a45acdf6ffb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-00