  <data key="d7">NGINX</data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Host Discovery">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Host Discovery</data>
  <data key="d1">attacktechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual Host Discovery is an action performed to identify virtual host names within a network environment, primarily through probing and analysis of server responses. This process involves examining how a target system responds to requests with different hostnames, leveraging name-based routing mechanisms to uncover hidden or unlisted virtual hosts. It is commonly used in reconnaissance and testing scenarios to map out the full scope of accessible services and domains hosted on a single IP address. The technique includes probing various hostnames, analyzing differences in server responses, and interpreting user-controlled input to infer the existence of virtual hosts. It may also involve testing accessible API endpoints and assessing local service reachability to detect virtual hosts that are not publicly documented. By probing the target system, this action enables the identification of virtual hosts through both direct hostname-based routing and indirect server response analysis. Virtual Host Discovery is a critical component of security assessments and network mapping, helping to reveal the complete attack surface of a web infrastructure.&lt;SEP&gt;An action that discovers virtual host names by interacting with name-based routing configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;An action that identifies accessible virtual hosts by exploiting name-based routing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-02d8de5bc4d7ef5b5730e6de21983a27-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4f1b727935e166da3087ad74c41475b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8ad4036a778fcc22e1513e5f0c0a8591-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">source-1bcb83f8254416e4.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-463bc40b9d3a7e65.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-4dbc50aa9b3261a0.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-12ff71155a138664.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-a9ccf5fa80acec79.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ec1ac76eae51358e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-edb2f8f9e3bad28b.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-f185a95c8594da55.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1787992262</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Virtual Host Discovery</data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Host Name">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Host Name</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual Host Name is a concrete object that contains the name of a virtual host, typically discovered during network reconnaissance or attack activities. It represents a virtual host name used in HTTP requests and is often obtained through host enumeration techniques. This artifact is specifically identified during security assessments, such as vulnerability scanning or penetration testing, where it serves as a key element in identifying accessible web services or hidden endpoints. The virtual host name may be extracted from server responses, such as HTTP headers, or discovered through techniques like DNS enumeration, subdomain brute-forcing, or directory crawling. As a concrete artifact, it is used in the context of reconnaissance to map out the attack surface of a target system. It can be produced or obtained during an attack, particularly in scenarios involving web application exploitation or infrastructure mapping. The discovery of such names may reveal internal systems, staging environments, or misconfigured domains that are not publicly exposed. Virtual Host Name is therefore a critical piece of intelligence in cybersecurity operations, enabling attackers or defenders to understand the structure of a web environment and assess potential vulnerabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;A concrete virtual host name discovered during reconnaissance.&lt;SEP&gt;A concrete object obtained during virtual host discovery.&lt;SEP&gt;A concrete name of a virtual host discovered during enumeration.&lt;SEP&gt;A concrete name identifying a virtual host discovered during an attack.&lt;SEP&gt;A concrete host name used to access internal services, obtained during an attack.&lt;SEP&gt;A concrete name of a virtual host discovered during the attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4689e3344d4cedcec51062aefaf0b4c4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c971a08d484b9763ebcf9e596a7147e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-02d8de5bc4d7ef5b5730e6de21983a27-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-02d8de5bc4d7ef5b5730e6de21983a27-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4f1b727935e166da3087ad74c41475b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4f1b727935e166da3087ad74c41475b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8ad4036a778fcc22e1513e5f0c0a8591-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8ad4036a778fcc22e1513e5f0c0a8591-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">source-1bcb83f8254416e4.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-463bc40b9d3a7e65.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-4dbc50aa9b3261a0.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-12ff71155a138664.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-a9ccf5fa80acec79.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ec1ac76eae51358e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-edb2f8f9e3bad28b.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-f185a95c8594da55.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1787992253</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Virtual Host Name</data>
</node>
<node id="PHP Filter Chain Payload">
  <data key="d0">PHP Filter Chain Payload</data>
  <data key="d1">payloadpattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A reusable payload pattern that chains PHP filters to bypass security controls.&lt;SEP&gt;A reusable payload pattern for exploiting PHP filter chains.&lt;SEP&gt;A reusable payload pattern that chains PHP filters to bypass input validation.&lt;SEP&gt;A reusable payload pattern using PHP filter chains to execute malicious code.&lt;SEP&gt;A reusable payload structure that chains PHP filters to bypass input validation.</data>
