<edge source="Command Output" target="JWT Token Forgery">
  <data key="d8">20.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Command Output-JWT Token Forgery refers to a technique in which the success or outcome of forging a JWT (JSON Web Token) is indicated through command output. The command output serves as a signal that the technique has been successfully executed, with multiple descriptions emphasizing that the output can indicate the success of the technique. This signal may manifest as a response, error message, or confirmation message generated by a system or tool during the execution of the forgery process. The command output can reflect whether the forged token was accepted, rejected, or processed in a specific manner, thereby providing feedback on the effectiveness of the attack. In some cases, the output may directly reveal the result of the JWT token forgery attempt, such as authentication success or failure. The technique generates command output signals that are used to assess the success of the forgery, and these signals are often observed in security testing or penetration testing environments. The command output can indicate the technique's success, and in certain instances, the technique itself can be inferred or confirmed through the nature of the output. The consistency across descriptions suggests that command output is a reliable indicator of the technique’s success, though the exact nature of the output may vary depending on the system under test.</data>
  <data key="d10">indicates, indicated by, can indicate, generates</data>
