<edge source="Credentials" target="Offline Password Cracking">
  <data key="d8">41.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Credentials-Offline Password Cracking is a technique that involves the extraction or generation of credentials as an artifact. This process typically occurs outside of a live system environment, allowing attackers to analyze and crack passwords without direct interaction with the target system. The technique can produce credentials in various forms, including plaintext passwords, hashed values, or other credential-related artifacts. These artifacts may result from the testing or exploitation of credential exposure, such as the use of stolen password databases or weak authentication mechanisms. The primary outcome of this technique is the creation of usable credentials, which can be leveraged for unauthorized access. The process may involve brute-force attacks, dictionary attacks, or the use of precomputed tables (e.g., rainbow tables) to reverse cryptographic hashes. The produced credentials can be stored, analyzed, or used to gain access to systems or accounts. While the technique is primarily associated with offensive security activities, it also highlights the importance of secure credential storage and protection mechanisms. The consistency across descriptions emphasizes that the generation of credentials as an artifact is a core outcome of this method.</data>
  <data key="d10">produced, can produce, tests, produces</data>
