<edge source="Credentials" target="Executable Upload Webshell">
  <data key="d8">18.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The relation Credentials-Executable Upload Webshell refers to a technique associated with the creation and handling of credentials within the context of webshell exploitation. This technique is capable of producing credentials as an artifact, which may include authentication tokens, usernames, passwords, or other access-related data. The production of credentials is a key outcome of this method, and it is consistently described across multiple sources as generating credentials in the form of a digital artifact. In some descriptions, the technique is noted to produce credentials as a specific type of artifact, emphasizing its role in the extraction or generation of authentication material. Additionally, the relation is sometimes described as consuming credentials, suggesting a dual functionality where it may both generate and utilize credentials in the process. The technique is also explicitly linked to the broader concept of executable upload webshells, which are used to gain unauthorized access to systems. The term "Credentials-Executable Upload Webshell" reflects the integration of credential generation with the act of uploading executable code to establish a webshell, thereby enabling persistent access. While the primary focus is on credential production, the potential for credential consumption indicates a more complex interaction within the attack lifecycle. The consistency across descriptions highlights the technique’s significance in cyber operations involving credential theft and system compromise.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique produces credentials as an artifact.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique can produce a credentials artifact.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique can produce credentials as an artifact.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique produces credentials artifact.</data>
  <data key="d10">can produce,consumes,produces</data>
