<edge source="Source Code Review" target="Source Code">
  <data key="d8">26.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Source Code-Source Code Review is a technique that both produces and consumes source code. The primary function of this technique is to generate source code as an artifact, with multiple descriptions emphasizing that it can produce source code artifacts, source code as a result, or source code as an artifact. These outputs are consistently referred to as artifacts, indicating that the technique's output is not merely code but a structured, formalized product. In addition to producing source code, the technique also consumes source code, suggesting that it operates on existing code as input to generate or refine new code artifacts. The process may involve analyzing, transforming, or validating the input source code to produce a refined or verified output. While the descriptions vary slightly in phrasing—some using "can produce" and others "produces"—the core functionality remains consistent: the technique both takes in source code and outputs source code artifacts. The term "Source Code Review" is explicitly linked to the production of the artifact "Source Code," indicating that this technique is likely used in software development or quality assurance contexts to evaluate and generate source code. Overall, the technique functions as a dual-purpose process, consuming existing source code and producing new or revised source code artifacts.</data>
  <data key="d10">can produce, consumes, produces</data>
