<edge source="Offline Password Cracking" target="Password Hash">
  <data key="d8">37.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Offline Password Cracking-Password Hash is a technique that involves the processing of password hashes, either as input or output, within the context of cybersecurity operations. The technique is primarily associated with the generation of password hash artifacts, which are produced as a result of the cracking process. Multiple descriptions indicate that the technique can produce password hash artifacts, with some variations in phrasing such as "produces a password hash," "produces password hashes," or "produces password hash as an artifact." These descriptions consistently affirm that the output of the technique includes password hash artifacts, emphasizing the creation of such data as a key outcome.

However, one description stands out by stating that the technique consumes password hashes, suggesting that it may also operate on existing password hash data as input. This implies a dual role: the technique both consumes password hashes (as part of the cracking process) and produces password hash artifacts (as a result of the cracking effort). The production of password hash artifacts may refer to the generation of new hash values, possibly from cracked passwords, or the extraction and representation of hashes from compromised systems. 

The consistency across most descriptions underscores that the primary function of Offline Password Cracking-Password Hash is to generate password hash artifacts, while the mention of consumption indicates that it may also process existing hashes during the cracking workflow. This dual capability highlights the technique's role in both analyzing and producing password hash data, depending on the operational context. The term "offline" suggests that the process occurs without real-time network interaction, typically using stored hash data for analysis. Overall, Offline Password Cracking-Password Hash is a method that both consumes and produces password hash artifacts, with a strong emphasis on the generation of hash data as a central output.</data>
  <data key="d10">produced, can produce, consumes, produces</data>
