<edge source="Offline Password Cracking" target="Protocol Allowlist">
  <data key="d8">17.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Offline Password Cracking-Protocol Allowlist is a defensive control mechanism designed to mitigate offline password cracking techniques by restricting the set of allowed network protocols. This control operates by permitting only specific, authorized protocols to be used, thereby limiting the attack surface available to adversaries. The protocol allowlist is intended to block or reduce the effectiveness of offline password cracking techniques, which often rely on exploiting specific communication protocols to gain unauthorized access. However, certain offline password cracking techniques can bypass this control, either by exploiting vulnerabilities in the allowlist implementation or by leveraging protocols that are permitted under the policy. While the allowlist is effective in defending against such attacks, its efficacy depends on the comprehensiveness and accuracy of the protocol restrictions. Some descriptions indicate that the technique may bypass the allowlist, suggesting potential weaknesses or workarounds. In cases where the allowlist is bypassed, the control's ability to prevent unauthorized access is diminished. The relationship between the technique and the allowlist is thus characterized by a dynamic interplay: the allowlist acts as a barrier, but the technique may circumvent it under certain conditions. The control's primary function is to block the offline password cracking technique by enforcing protocol restrictions, but its success is contingent on the sophistication of the attack and the robustness of the allowlist configuration.</data>
  <data key="d10">blocks, defends against, bypassed by, bypasses, mitigates, may bypass, reduces effectiveness of</data>
