  <data key="d16">Linux Host</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Executable Upload Webshell" target="Credential Exposure">
  <data key="d8">14.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The (Credential Exposure, Executable Upload Webshell) relation describes a technique in which an executable upload webshell is used to exploit vulnerabilities related to credential exposure. This technique involves uploading a malicious executable file to a target system, often through a web interface, with the intent of gaining unauthorized access. The primary objective of the technique is to leverage weaknesses where credentials are exposed, such as through insecure storage, misconfigurations, or insecure transmission. By exploiting these credential exposure vulnerabilities, attackers can obtain sensitive authentication data, which they then use to compromise systems or escalate privileges. The technique is specifically associated with the credential exposure vulnerability class, targeting systems where credentials are inadequately protected. It may involve testing for the presence of such vulnerabilities before exploitation. The executable upload webshell serves as a method to deliver and execute malicious code, enabling persistent access and control over the compromised system. This relation highlights the interplay between a specific attack technique and a broader class of security weaknesses, emphasizing the importance of securing credential storage and access mechanisms to prevent exploitation.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique tests or exploits credential exposure vulnerabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;The vulnerability class is exploited by the executable upload webshell technique.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique exploits credential exposure to obtain credentials.</data>
  <data key="d10">exploits,targets,tests or exploits</data>
  <data key="d11">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67f151b5c1ed66e0d6be294a338e261a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2c01c9361375f50dd8673c9eb15cacd0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ca4ebbe397b88a5e1985154047a1fd42-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b7c72285e15bcd2791980b58faff89f6-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b7c72285e15bcd2791980b58faff89f6-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b9ddc793324d868b5ed0d90f4c192307-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d12">source-1bcb83f8254416e4.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-429f1cea7765f3e2.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ea557e757697285d.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-4c91a7e4da4eea3a.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-67d16a04c4799edd.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-516d729c800024a5.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-7e31c39a962ccdd6.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-370982d0d06e0982.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-eae76c910437f664.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ec1ac76eae51358e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-f43f768c997245d2.md</data>
