<edge source="JWT Token Forgery" target="Password Hash">
  <data key="d8">13.0</data>
  <data key="d9">(JWT Token Forgery, Password Hash) is a technique associated with the generation of password hash artifacts. Multiple descriptions indicate that this technique can produce password hashes, with some specifying the output as a "password hash artifact." The consistent emphasis across the descriptions is that the technique results in the creation of password hash data, which may be used as an artifact in security contexts. The term "artifact" suggests that the output may be stored, extracted, or utilized in a structured form, potentially for forensic analysis or system validation. While the descriptions vary slightly in phrasing—some using "can produce" and others "produces"—they all converge on the same core function: the generation of password hash outputs. There is no indication of conflicting interpretations or multiple distinct entities; rather, the variations appear to reflect stylistic differences in describing the same technical capability. Therefore, the technique is understood as one that reliably generates password hash artifacts as part of its operational outcome.</data>
  <data key="d10">can produce,produced,produces</data>
