</edge>
<edge source="JWT Token Forgery" target="Execute Code">
  <data key="d8">12.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The relation (Execute Code, JWT Token Forgery) refers to a technique that supports the goal of executing code, particularly within the context of security attacks. This technique enables an adversary to achieve code execution by exploiting vulnerabilities related to JWT (JSON Web Token) token forgery. By manipulating or forging JWT tokens—often through weaknesses in token validation, signing algorithms, or key management—an attacker can gain unauthorized access to systems or services. Once authenticated, the forged token can be used to execute arbitrary code on the target system, effectively bypassing security controls. This method is commonly leveraged in scenarios where JWT tokens are used for authentication and authorization, and the implementation fails to properly validate the integrity or authenticity of the tokens. The technique can support the broader attack goal of executing code, making it a significant concern in applications that rely on JWT-based authentication. While the relation is primarily described in terms of enabling code execution, the specific mechanisms may vary depending on the implementation flaws present in the system. The effectiveness of this technique hinges on the ability to forge valid tokens that are accepted by the target application, thereby granting access to privileged operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique supports testing for the goal of executing code.&lt;SEP&gt;The goal supports achieving the goal of executing code.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique supports executing code by forging valid tokens.</data>
  <data key="d10">achieves,can support,supports,supports achieving,supports testing for</data>
