<edge source="JWT Token Forgery" target="SQL Comment Auth Bypass Payload">
  <data key="d8">11.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The (JWT Token Forgery, SQL Comment Auth Bypass Payload) relation describes a technical interaction involving two distinct but potentially overlapping security techniques. The first aspect of this relation is the use of a payload in the context of JWT token forgery, where the payload supports or is utilized in the process of forging JSON Web Tokens. This technique typically involves manipulating the token’s structure or cryptographic signature to gain unauthorized access, and the payload plays a central role in enabling such forgery. The payload pattern is specifically designed to target JWT token forgery, indicating its structured form is aligned with exploiting weaknesses in token validation mechanisms.

The second aspect of the relation involves the use of SQL comment auth bypass payloads, which are employed to circumvent authentication checks by exploiting SQL injection vulnerabilities, particularly through the insertion of comment syntax to manipulate query logic. This technique uses a specific payload pattern to bypass authentication mechanisms in database-driven applications. While the two techniques—JWT token forgery and SQL comment auth bypass—are distinct in their attack vectors and targets, the relation suggests a scenario where a payload may be associated with both, either through shared exploitation strategies or in a layered attack context. The payload pattern is thus described as being relevant to both JWT token forgery and SQL comment auth bypass, indicating a potential convergence in offensive security practices where the same payload or its structural design can serve dual purposes across different vulnerability types. The relation highlights a hybrid or multi-stage attack approach, where payload design bridges multiple exploit methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The payload pattern is used in the technique.&lt;SEP&gt;The payload pattern is used in JWT token forgery.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique uses the SQL comment auth bypass payload.</data>
