<edge source="URL Userinfo Confusion Payload" target="WAF Or Request Filter">
  <data key="d8">8.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The relation (URL Userinfo Confusion Payload, WAF Or Request Filter) describes a cybersecurity interaction involving a specific type of payload and a defensive mechanism. The URL userinfo confusion payload is a pattern designed to exploit or circumvent security controls implemented by a Web Application Firewall (WAF) or request filter. These controls are intended to detect and block malicious input, particularly payloads that manipulate the userinfo component of a URL—such as those using unusual or encoded characters in the username or password section of a URL. The payload pattern is capable of bypassing these WAF or request filter controls, thereby evading detection and potentially enabling unauthorized access or exploitation. While the control is designed to block such payloads, the specific nature of the URL userinfo confusion allows the payload to bypass these defenses. In some cases, the control may successfully block the payload when properly enforced, but the payload's design enables it to exploit weaknesses or misconfigurations in the filtering logic. The relation highlights a dynamic where the payload's ability to bypass security controls presents a challenge to the effectiveness of WAFs and request filters, particularly when dealing with non-standard URL structures. The interaction underscores the need for updated detection rules and deeper analysis of URL parsing behaviors to prevent such bypass techniques.</data>
  <data key="d10">blocks,bypasses</data>
  <data key="d11">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6e8b98451fa828a77a410acce1592cf2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-da0c7cc16e444f7a837833f0b1605208-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ca4ebbe397b88a5e1985154047a1fd42-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4f1b727935e166da3087ad74c41475b-chunk-001</data>
