<edge source="Wildcard Command Filter Bypass" target="WAF Or Request Filter">
  <data key="d8">15.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The WAF Or Request Filter-Wildcard Command Filter Bypass refers to a security vulnerability or attack technique in which a payload pattern is designed to circumvent the defensive mechanisms of a Web Application Firewall (WAF) or request filtering system. These systems typically employ wildcard filters to detect and block malicious input patterns, such as those associated with command injection or other code execution attacks. However, certain payload patterns can exploit weaknesses in how these filters are implemented, allowing them to evade detection. The bypass occurs when the payload is structured in a way that the WAF or request filter fails to recognize it as malicious, often due to subtle variations in syntax, encoding, or the use of specific character sequences that are not properly accounted for in the filter logic. In some cases, the control mechanism may successfully block payloads that attempt to exploit wildcard command filter bypasses, indicating that the effectiveness of the bypass depends on the specific configuration and rule set of the WAF. Despite this, multiple descriptions confirm that the payload pattern may, can, or does bypass the WAF or request filter control, particularly when the control relies on wildcard-based detection. The term "WAF Or Request Filter-Wildcard Command Filter Bypass" thus describes a scenario where an attacker leverages such evasion techniques to penetrate security defenses, potentially leading to unauthorized access or execution of malicious commands. The consistency across descriptions suggests a well-documented method of bypassing these controls, though the exact nature of the payload and the conditions under which it succeeds may vary depending on the implementation of the filtering system.</data>
  <data key="d10">may bypass, bypasses, blocks, can bypass</data>
  <data key="d11">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0584c585862d9a9383e3c2512964e051-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-becce52057339c535889e197f6df8c45-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-becce52057339c535889e197f6df8c45-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6e8b98451fa828a77a410acce1592cf2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1460b2cac00bc568a22b020dc995d48d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f480ea9fcbb09d97e0819addf2df923b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-da0c7cc16e444f7a837833f0b1605208-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ca4ebbe397b88a5e1985154047a1fd42-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b9fbca654baa1f52872a4a45acdf6ffb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4f1b727935e166da3087ad74c41475b-chunk-001</data>
