<edge source="WAF Or Request Filter" target="SQL Injection File Read">
  <data key="d8">10.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The technique known as SQL Injection File Read refers to a method used in cybersecurity to exploit vulnerabilities in web applications by injecting SQL queries that allow unauthorized access to files on the server. This technique is often employed by attackers to extract sensitive data, such as configuration files or credentials, from the underlying system. The effectiveness of this technique is frequently evaluated in relation to defensive controls, particularly Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) or request filters. These controls are designed to detect and block malicious SQL injection attempts, including those aimed at reading files. While some descriptions indicate that the WAF or request filter may block the SQL Injection File Read technique—thereby preventing unauthorized file access—other descriptions suggest that the technique can bypass these defenses, highlighting potential weaknesses in the implementation or configuration of such controls. The inconsistency in the descriptions may reflect differing environments or configurations where the defensive mechanisms vary in their ability to detect and mitigate the attack. In some cases, the control successfully blocks the technique by identifying suspicious query patterns or forbidden file access attempts. However, in other scenarios, the technique may evade detection due to obfuscation techniques, such as encoding or use of alternative SQL syntax, allowing it to bypass the WAF or request filter. Therefore, the behavior of the SQL Injection File Read technique in relation to WAF or request filter controls is context-dependent, with outcomes ranging from successful blocking to successful bypass. This variability underscores the importance of robust, up-to-date security configurations and continuous monitoring to counter evolving attack methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique can bypass WAF or request filter defenses.</data>
  <data key="d10">blocks,bypasses,may be blocked by,may bypass</data>
  <data key="d11">doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-774dd7b69cee60333fe159b91c5115dc-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4a49f72fdeac26b85fd80fd22bf2c4b9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f6a368a385f7056f3a4e83a6daacc92e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8ad4036a778fcc22e1513e5f0c0a8591-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d12">source-1bcb83f8254416e4.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-045067d746e8d66c.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-516d729c800024a5.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-0d4e246da395857e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-2e266fadcba1fc0d.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ec1ac76eae51358e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-f185a95c8594da55.md</data>
  <data key="d13">1787992383</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
  <data key="d15">SQL Injection File Read</data>
  <data key="d16">WAF Or Request Filter</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WAF Or Request Filter" target="SSRF Localhost Probing">
  <data key="d8">13.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The SSRF Localhost Probing-WAF Or Request Filter relation describes a cybersecurity technique known as SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) localhost probing, which involves exploiting a web application to make requests to internal resources by targeting the localhost. This technique is primarily used to bypass WAF (Web Application Firewall) or request filter controls, which are designed to prevent unauthorized or malicious network requests. Multiple descriptions indicate that the SSRF localhost probing technique may bypass these defensive controls, allowing attackers to probe internal systems that are not directly accessible from external networks. Some descriptions emphasize that the technique can successfully evade WAF or request filter defenses, while others note that such controls may block the probing attempts. This suggests that the effectiveness of the bypass depends on the specific configuration and capabilities of the WAF or request filter in place. The relation highlights a common security challenge where attackers leverage SSRF vulnerabilities to test or exploit internal services, potentially leading to information disclosure or further compromise. The technique's ability to bypass security controls underscores the importance of robust input validation, network segmentation, and monitoring for unusual internal traffic patterns. While some descriptions present the technique as a reliable method for circumventing defenses, others acknowledge that defensive mechanisms may mitigate or block the probing, indicating variability in real-world outcomes based on the environment and implementation.</data>
  <data key="d10">blocks, bypasses, may bypass, may be blocked by</data>
  <data key="d11">doc-67f151b5c1ed66e0d6be294a338e261a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20e319f206fb018ed527c5b31c09ddf0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5e4ee546aa8f7ecfb659ca7fb68e9be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1a922685f9944c8d348cfa4732a95f15-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c8df9b099194a66605da7b496d31a4a5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b7c72285e15bcd2791980b58faff89f6-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f6a368a385f7056f3a4e83a6daacc92e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-880110b45de32b68e00014bf0300aced-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3e8a9899a4d34db71efbd953d99c628b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4f1b727935e166da3087ad74c41475b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8ad4036a778fcc22e1513e5f0c0a8591-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d12">source-ea557e757697285d.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-67d16a04c4799edd.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-045067d746e8d66c.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-bae40e62361a8519.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-0d4e246da395857e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-370982d0d06e0982.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-2e266fadcba1fc0d.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-eae76c910437f664.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-ec1ac76eae51358e.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-edb2f8f9e3bad28b.md&lt;SEP&gt;source-f185a95c8594da55.md</data>
