<edge source="Linux Host" target="SQL Injection File Read">
  <data key="d8">33.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Linux Host-SQL Injection File Read is a technique that exploits SQL injection vulnerabilities within environments where Linux Host is the underlying technology stack. This technique specifically targets Linux host environments, leveraging the presence of a Linux Host system as a critical component of the attack surface. It is applicable when a Linux host is present, and the technique relies on the Linux Host technology stack to execute the file read operation. The method uses Linux Host as part of the target technology stack, often utilizing the Linux host environment to access or retrieve files through SQL injection pathways. The technique is relevant to the Linux Host technology stack and is designed to exploit vulnerabilities that allow unauthorized file access in systems running Linux Host. It targets Linux hosts, Linux host environments, and the Linux Host system itself, using the Linux Host as the primary target or platform for the attack. The technique is closely tied to the Linux Host infrastructure, with its effectiveness dependent on the configuration and security posture of the Linux Host system. The use of Linux Host technology in the target environment enables the execution of SQL Injection File Read, making it a significant concern for systems relying on this technology stack.</data>
  <data key="d10">supports, is relevant to, relevant to, uses, targets, applies when</data>
