<edge source="Linux Host" target="SSRF Localhost Probing">
  <data key="d8">16.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The technique known as Linux Host-SSRF Localhost Probing is a cybersecurity method that leverages the Linux host environment to exploit Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerabilities. This technique specifically targets Linux hosts, utilizing the Linux host technology stack as a foundational component of its execution. It operates within the Linux host environment, relying on the system's configuration and network capabilities to probe localhost resources. The technique uses a Linux host as part of its operational framework, and it can run on such systems to perform internal network reconnaissance. The Linux host technology stack supports the SSRF localhost probing technique, enabling attackers to exploit misconfigurations or insecure service endpoints accessible from within the host. This method is relevant to the Linux host technology stack and is often used to bypass network restrictions by making requests from the internal network perspective. The technique targets the Linux host environment, emphasizing the role of the host in facilitating internal probing. It is applicable across various Linux-based systems where SSRF vulnerabilities exist, particularly in environments where applications allow untrusted input to initiate network requests. The technique exploits the trust relationship between services running on the same Linux host, enabling attackers to access services bound to localhost that are not exposed externally. This form of probing is significant in penetration testing and red team operations, where understanding internal network topology is crucial. The consistency across descriptions indicates that the technique is primarily focused on leveraging Linux hosts as both the platform and target for SSRF-based localhost reconnaissance.</data>
  <data key="d10">supports, relevant to, is relevant to, uses, targets, runs on</data>
